Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.37,0:00:05.26,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A generated snapshot has no\Ntrue twin, so we compare crowds.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.68,0:00:08.29,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Picture each crowd as a pile of sand.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.60,0:00:12.44,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A {\c&H00A1ED&}distribution distance{\c&HF7F4F2&} scores\Nhow different the two piles are.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.81,0:00:17.51,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}The earth-mover kind counts how\Nmuch sand must move, and how far.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.25,0:00:22.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}A perfect model, scored against\Nthe data: will it get zero?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.85,0:00:30.96,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}No. Roll a fair die a thousand\Ntimes, then a thousand more.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.20,0:00:35.65,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Same die, same truth,\Nyet the two histograms differ.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.14,0:00:40.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}That gap is the {\c&H00A1ED&}noise floor{\c&HF7F4F2&},\Nabout what a perfect model scores.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.23,0:00:46.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Judge every model against it,\Nat equal sample size, never against zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.61,0:00:51.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}One catch: the samples\Nmust be independent.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.43,0:00:55.92,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,100)\move(960,1020,960,1016,0,120)}Overlap them, and the floor drops\Ntoo low, as the report warns.
